From 9f6b64aba05ce8851a44aeebb65f17c046e5bc52 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 19:35:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] rtrafd: attribute IPv6 flows per device; label the
 upload-only class chart

poll_conntrack() skipped every non-ipv4 conntrack line, so a client's IPv6
traffic reached the WAN and per-network totals but no device row. On a box with
a delegated prefix that is most of a modern client's traffic: a field RT-BE88U
showed 978 Mb/s on the WAN line and 521 kb/s against the device pulling it.

v4 finds the device by looking the LAN source up in /proc/net/arp. v6 has no
such file and no NAT, so either end of a flow may be the local device. Both are
now resolved against the kernel's IPv6 neighbour cache (netlink RTM_GETNEIGH,
bridge ports only - a neighbour on ppp0 is the ISP's router, not a client) and
credited to the MAC, the same key the v4 path uses, so a dual-stack device is
one row rather than two. Router-sourced v6 goes to the Router bucket via the
addresses in /proc/net/if_inet6. Per-network rings pick up v6 for free.

The flow key and the byte deltas stay on the RAW conntrack tuple; only the
presentation is swapped when the device is the reply side. If the key tracked
the credited end, a neighbour entry expiring would change it, the fl->key test
would miss, and the flow's whole cumulative counter would be credited as a
single delta.

cli_t.ip is left alone: it is a 16-byte IPv4 field inside the persisted
dbhdr_t, so widening it for a v6 literal would change the on-disk layout and
discard every saved history file (the DB_VER 1->2 precedent). A dual-stack
device keeps its v4 label. top_t.src is widened - top talkers are not
persisted.

Also: the "By QoS class" panel on the Analyzer now carries an "Upload only"
badge (RTRF_75/76, English-seeded in all 25 packs). That engine shapes the
upload direction only, so the chart can never show a streaming device's
download, and the caveat was buried in a FAQ answer.

RTRF_45 rewritten in all 25 packs: it still described the pre-v2.3.3
accelerator-table source and told users IPv6 was not split per-device, which is
now the opposite of what the code does. The 24 translations of the old text are
discarded rather than left wrong; retranslation owed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
